# ==============================================================================
# PipelineShield Platform Configuration
# ==============================================================================
# Hard Security Rules:
# 1. NEVER commit real secrets or tokens to source control.
# 2. These variables are read strictly server-side by Next.js API routes
#    (/api/latest-report, /api/webhook/github, /api/local-ai).
# 3. No secrets are ever exposed to browser client bundles (no NEXT_PUBLIC_*).
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. GitHub Integration & Ingestion
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GITHUB_TOKEN:
# Personal Access Token (classic or fine-grained) or GitHub App Installation Token.
# Used server-side to query workflow runs and download the 'security-summary-reports' artifact zip.
#
# Required Scopes:
#   - Fine-grained token:
#       * Repository permissions: Actions (Read), Metadata (Read)
#   - Classic token:
#       * 'repo' (private repo) or 'public_repo' (public repo)
#
# Tradeoff Note (Personal Access Token vs. GitHub App):
#   - Personal Access Token (PAT): Quickest to set up for single-developer or staging environments.
#     Tokens are tied to an individual user account and require manual rotation.
#   - GitHub App (Recommended for Production): Provides fine-grained repository-scoped permissions,
#     short-lived auto-refreshing installation tokens (1 hour expiry), organization ownership,
#     and independent webhook delivery tracking without tying to personal user accounts.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# GITHUB_REPOSITORY:
# Target repository in 'owner/repo' format.
# Defaults to "YogamruthReddy/PipelineShield" if omitted.
GITHUB_REPOSITORY=YogamruthReddy/PipelineShield

# GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET:
# Secret key configured in GitHub Repository Settings -> Webhooks.
# Used server-side to cryptographically verify HMAC SHA-256 signatures (X-Hub-Signature-256)
# on incoming 'workflow_run.completed' events.
#
# ==============================================================================
# Dual-Webhook Strategy (Local Development vs. Production Deployed App):
# GitHub supports registering multiple webhooks for the same repository.
# Maintain two separate webhook registrations with distinct secrets:
#
# Configuration A — Local Development (Tunnel Mode):
#   GitHub cannot send webhooks directly to 'localhost'. Expose the receiver via tunnel:
#   1. Start dev server: cd dashboard && npm run dev (http://localhost:3000)
#   2. Expose via tunnel:
#        Cloudflare: cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3000
#        ngrok:      ngrok http 3000
#   3. In GitHub -> Settings -> Webhooks -> Add webhook:
#        Payload URL:      https://<YOUR-TUNNEL-DOMAIN>/api/webhook/github
#        Content type:     application/json
#        Secret:           <A dedicated random secret for local dev in .env.local>
#        SSL verification: Enabled
#        Events:           Select "Workflow runs"
#        Active:           Checked
#   4. Note: Local webhook triggers only while your local server and tunnel are active.
#
# Configuration B — Production Deployed App:
#   When PipelineShield is deployed to production:
#   1. Add a second webhook in GitHub with the deployed domain:
#        Payload URL:      https://<your-production-app.domain>/api/webhook/github
#        Content type:     application/json
#        Secret:           <A separate, high-entropy secret configured in cloud env>
#        SSL verification: Enabled
#        Events:           Select "Workflow runs"
#        Active:           Checked
#   2. Never reuse temporary local tunnel URLs for production webhooks.
# ==============================================================================
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Local AI Security Assistant (Optional & Advisory-Only)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Local AI assistant runs locally on the operator's machine (e.g. via Ollama).
# Hard boundary: The CI security gate workflow and deterministic policy engine remain
# 100% authoritative. Local AI is strictly advisory and cannot override verdicts.

# LOCAL_AI_URL:
# Base URL for local Ollama runtime. Defaults to "http://127.0.0.1:11434"
LOCAL_AI_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434

# LOCAL_AI_MODEL:
# Name of locally pulled model (e.g. qwen2.5:3b, llama3.2:3b). Defaults to "qwen2.5:3b"
LOCAL_AI_MODEL=qwen2.5:3b

# LOCAL_AI_TIMEOUT_MS:
# Request timeout in milliseconds when querying local model runtime. Defaults to 30000.
LOCAL_AI_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
